Every autumn, in the days before Navaratri, my mother's kitchen would be scrubbed, the old spices thrown out, the brass polished. Nobody called it therapy. But the feeling afterwards, of a house with room to breathe, is exactly what these nine nights can offer the mind.

If your mind has felt heavy lately, or restless, or unusually hard on you, this post is for you. Not as a patient, not as a project. Just as a person who would like to feel a little lighter by the tenth day.

## Before we begin: three qualities, none of them bad

Ayurveda does not divide the mind into healthy and unhealthy. It describes three qualities that colour every mind, every day:

- **Tamas (heaviness)** The quality that lets you sleep, rest, and stop. Too much of it, for too long, and you feel stuck, foggy, unable to begin.
- **Rajas (restlessness)** The quality that gets you out of bed and into the world. Too much of it and you cannot stop: craving, arguing, chasing.
- **Sattva (clarity)** The quality that lets you see what is actually true, and respond rather than react.

Charaka put it in one line that Ayurvedic students still learn by heart:

> वायुः पित्तं कफश्चोक्तः शारीरो दोषसङ्ग्रहः।
> मानसः पुनरुद्दिष्टो रजश्च तम एव च॥
>
> "Vayu, pitta and kapha are the bodily doshas; rajas and tamas are the mental ones." ([Charaka Samhita, Sutrasthana 1/57])

Notice what is not on the list. Sattva is never called a dosha. Clarity cannot be in excess. The whole journey below is simply this: let heaviness and restlessness settle, so that the clarity which was always there can show itself.

## Three goddesses, three stages of the journey

The nine nights are traditionally grouped in threes, each under one form of the Goddess. Read them as three stages of the same walk.

### Nights 1–3: Mahakali, who ends the darkness

She who devours time itself, fierce because she loves us; she does not scold the darkness, she simply ends it.

This is the stage of heaviness. Her gift is not insight, it is energy: enough to get up, open the window, and look at what has been avoided. If you are in a low phase, do not aim for more than this.

### Nights 4–6: Mahalakshmi, who brings enough

Golden one on the lotus, giver of abundance; she teaches that true wealth is a heart that no longer says "more".

This is the stage of restlessness. Craving, anger and greed are all forms of hunger. Her gift is the quiet feeling of having enough, which is the only thing that truly calms a restless mind.

### Nights 7–9: Mahasaraswati, who clears the sight

Clad in white, holding the veena and the book; she removes the veil, and we see ourselves without distortion.

This is the stage of clarity. The burdens here are subtle: pride, envy, and the small "I" that takes everything personally. They appear precisely because you have already come a long way.

## Manas Bhāvas (Mental States) - the 10 steps

One night, one burden, one small practice. The Sanskrit terms are from Ayurveda's own list of mind-based conditions ([Charak Samhita New Edition, Manas]). The "how it may feel" column is written for you, not about you; it is my reading, not a diagnosis.

| # | Guṇa (quality) | Burden (Sanskrit + English) | How it may feel to you | A small practice |
| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | Tamas (heaviness) | Ālasya (lethargy) | Glāni (weariness), Jaḍatā (mental dullness), and Suppression: you feel drained or shut down, keep emotions inside, and struggle to get started. | Keep one daily routine steady. |
| 2 | Tamas (heaviness) | Bhaya (fear) | Cintā (worry), Śaṅkā (doubt or apprehension), Udvega (anxiety or agitation), and Insecurity: uncertainty feeds worry; you stay on edge and find it hard to feel safe. | Speak kindly to yourself. |
| 3 | Tamas (heaviness) | Moha (confusion or delusion) | Bhrama (bewilderment), Śoka (grief), Viṣāda (despondency), Heartbreak, and Trauma: painful experiences may leave you disoriented, weighed down by loss, or unable to make sense of what happened. | Reflect gently; do not force memories. |
| 4 | Rajas (restlessness) | Kāma (desire or craving) | Utsukatā (eagerness or restless anticipation) and Āvega (emotional surge or agitation): longing builds into impatience, and you feel pulled to act quickly or seek relief. | Pause before acting on urges. |
| 5 | Rajas (restlessness) | Krodha (anger) | Betrayal, Amarṣa (resentment or intolerance of an affront), Ghṛṇā (aversion or disgust), Ugratā (fierceness), and Raudra (wrathfulness): feeling hurt or wronged may turn into resentment, rejection, or an urge to lash out. | Pause; speak truthfully and gently. |
| 6 | Rajas (restlessness) | Lobha (greed) | Kārpaṇya (miserliness or a sense of inadequacy): you fear not having enough, cling to what you have, and find it difficult to let go or share. | Give a little, within your means. |
| 7 | Sattva (clarity) | Mada (pride or arrogance) | Māna (self-importance) and Dambha (pretence or ostentation): you seek recognition, defend your image, or hide vulnerability behind a confident front. | Admit one uncertainty honestly. |
| 8 | Sattva (clarity) | Mātsarya (envy or unwillingness to share) | Īrṣyā (jealousy) and Abhyasūyā (fault-finding or resentment): comparison leaves you feeling diminished; others’ success becomes difficult to appreciate. | Appreciate one quality in another. |
| 9 | Sattva (clarity) | Ahaṅkāra (ego or sense of self) | Shame, Guilt, Dainya (dejection), Abandonment, Loneliness, Helplessness, Hopelessness, and Vilāpa (lamentation): you blame yourself, feel left behind or alone, and struggle to see a way forward; distress may spill into crying or grieving aloud. | Be gentle; seek trusted support. |
| 10 | Beyond the three Guṇas (qualities) | Balance | Harṣa (joy) and Prīti (affection or delight): you feel warmth and connection. Balance means having room for both pleasant and difficult feelings without reacting immediately. | Notice feelings without judging. |

## Why this order, and why it is kind

Why not start with the "big" ones, ego and pride? Because a heavy mind cannot even see its own cravings, let alone its ego. Heaviness has to lift first. Then restlessness can calm. Only then do the subtle burdens become visible at all.

This order is also a kindness. If you reach night 7 and suddenly notice your own pride, you have not got worse. You have got clearer. The Goddess of wisdom does not show you anything you are not ready to see.

## A closer look at night 9: the three faces of "I"

We usually picture ego as arrogance. Ayurveda sees it more gently, as ahankara, "the I-maker", something every person needs in order to say "this is me, this is mine." It only causes pain when it tilts.

- **Steady:** "I am part of the whole." Self-respect, clear boundaries, no need to prove anything.
- **Inflated:** "I am separate, better or worse." Comparison, loneliness, needing to win.
- **Deflated:** "I do not matter." Neglecting your own care, apologising for existing.

If your struggle is the deflated kind, night 9 is not about becoming smaller. You have been small long enough. It is about remembering that you belong.


## How to actually do this

A few honest suggestions from someone who has tried and failed at ambitious festival plans.

- **One practice a night, not nine.** The table is a menu, not a checklist. Pick the row that made you wince a little; that one is yours.
- **Keep the body in it.** Ayurveda never treats the mind alone. Warm, fresh, simply cooked food, eaten at regular times, does more for a heavy or restless mind than most people expect. You are what you digest, and that includes the day's feelings.
- **Watch, don't judge.** Charaka's name for mind-care is sattvavajaya, which he defines simply as "withdrawing the mind from harmful objects" [Charaka Samhita, Sutrasthana 11/54]. Not fighting the thought. Just gently turning away from it, again and again, the way you would guide a child away from a hot stove.
- **Let the ritual be a ritual.** The lamp, the mantra, the fasting if you keep it: these belong to what Ayurveda calls spiritual care. They are allowed to simply comfort you.
- **Expect to slip.** Night 4 you will overeat, or snap at someone on night 5. That is not failure. That is the burden showing itself so that you can see it. Begin again the next evening.

## The tenth day: what remains

The tenth day has no burden assigned to it, and that is the point. It is not a new achievement. It is what remains when the nine have loosened their grip.

Ayurveda's word for health is swastha, "settled in oneself". Not someone who never feels heavy or restless, but someone who notices, chooses, and comes back to rest. You may not be fully there on day ten. But you may notice you are a little closer than you were on day one, and that is worth lighting a lamp for.

If you want to understand where these nine burdens come from, how Ayurveda explains the mind's machinery, and what deeper care looks like, I have written a companion post: How Ayurveda Understands the Mind.

## Closing

You do not have to carry all nine burdens at once, and you do not have to set them all down this week. One small practice a night is enough. May these nine nights leave you lighter in body and clearer in mind. Shubh Navaratri.

*This is educational content, not medical advice. If low mood, anxiety or any mental health concern stays with you, please speak to a qualified Ayurvedic physician or psychiatrist. Reaching out is itself a step toward clarity.*

